hET":"Inclusive Design and PhET","inclusiveDesign.about.ourGoalIsToCreate":"Our goal is to create the most enjoyable inclusive learning experiences at scale. When learners can explore through seeing, hearing, touching, and feeling, new opportunities for rich and adaptable learning experiences emerge.","inclusiveDesign.about.teamVideoTitle":"Discover the PhET Inclusive Design Team: Innovating Accessible Education","inclusiveDesign.about.inclusivelyDesignedPhETSimulations":"Inclusively designed PhET simulations are each built with a <strong>multimodal</strong> model; these models can express objects, states, relationships, and changes through visual, auditory, and tactile features.","inclusiveDesign.about.inclusiveFeaturesPositionWhatLearners":"<a {{0}}>Inclusive features</a> position what learners can see, hear, and feel, onto equal footing – this <strong>modal parity</strong> results in learning tools that can:","inclusiveDesign.about.beAsEffectiveNonVisually":"be as effective non-visually as visually.","inclusiveDesign.about.empowerSensoriallyDiverseLearners":"empower sensorially diverse learners.","inclusiveDesign.about.resultInNewLessScreenCentric":"result in new, less screen-centric, ways to learn with PhET sims.","inclusiveDesign.about.asPhetEvolvesAndScales":"As PhET evolves and scales inclusive design, inclusive features, and their implementation, more simulations will be available with more inclusive features. Not all features can be made immediately available to all simulations. Some features are more complex and resource-intensive than others. Each feature has a different timeline for broader implementation.","inclusiveDesign.about.ourInclusiveDesignAndResearchProcess":"Our Inclusive Design and Research Process","inclusiveDesign.about.ideasAndCollaboration":"<strong>IDEAS & COLLABORATION:</strong> Start with a science or math concept where learning benefits from rich multimodal exploration, and collaborate with partners.","inclusiveDesign.about.prototyping":"<strong>PROTOTYPING:</strong> Synchronously prototype multimodal features, exploring what you will see, hear, and feel, during simulation use. This process is like solving a complex multidimensional puzzle, and explores how the modalities can both stand alone and work together to support learning.","inclusiveDesign.about.collaborativeInvestigation":"<strong>COLLABORATIVE INVESTIGATION:</strong> Collaboratively investigate and expand prototypes with learners. Through a combination of individual interviews, discussions, and co-design sessions we closely observe what learners need, what they do, and what they perceive through interaction. We share ideas and discuss, and sometimes even co-create ideas directly with learners. We work with learners with diverse needs, including learners with sensory and learning differences. We aim to work with learners and their teachers across the full spectrum of human sensory, mobility, and cognitive diversity.","inclusiveDesign.about.refining":"<strong>REFINING:</strong> We refine and polish, designing solutions to the challenges and entanglements that occur at the intersections of modalities. We make difficult design decisions about what design elements to include and what to let go of. A final version of the simulation is put through multiple rounds of rigorous quality assurance testing across a wide range of platforms and devices. Bugs are squashed, and the finalized simulation is published to the PhET website.","inclusiveDesign.about.phetsUniqueOpenSourceSoftware":"<strong>PhET’s unique open source software architecture</strong> supports inclusive design processes and integration of multimodal interactions. Check out our <a {{0}}>open source community</a> of developers creating inclusive web media using PhET code libraries.","inclusiveDesign.about.teamsAndPartners":"Teams and Partners","inclusiveDesign.about.phetsTeamOfDesigners":"PhET’s team of designers and developers have developed specialized skills in creating multimodal features for highly interactive experiences. ThePhET team is committed to advancing their knowledge of inclusive practices. Collaborating with partners with unique expertise helps explore new aspects of Inclusive Design. Current partners include:","inclusiveDesign.about.chromeLab":"<a {{0}}>CHROME Lab, St. Louis University</a>","inclusiveDesign.about.embodiedDesignResearchLaboratory":"<a {{0}}>Embodied Design Research Laboratory</a>","inclusiveDesign.about.inclusiveDesignResearchCentre":"<a {{0}}>Inclusive Design Research Centre</a>","inclusiveDesign.about.sonificationLab":"<a {{0}}>Sonification Lab</a>","inclusiveDesign.about.benetech":"<a {{0}}>Benetech</a> and the <a {{1}}>DIAGRAM+ Center</a>"}}},features:{"InclusiveDesignFeaturesPanel.tsx":function(e,t,n){let i,o,a,r,s,l,c,u,m,d,p,h,g,y;n.export({InclusiveDesignFeaturesPanel:()=>b},!0),n.link("react",{default(e){i=e},useRef(e){o=e}},0),n.link("../../../../../api/data/constants",{IMG_ROOT(e){a=e},OPTIONAL_METEOR_PREFIX(e){r=e}},1),n.link("../../../../../api/strings/useStrings",{useStrings(e){s=e}},2),n.link("../../../../../api/urlUtils.js",{getInlineExternalHref(e){l=e},getInlineHref(e){c=e}},3),n.link("../../../../components/disclosure/DisclosureList",{DisclosureList(e){u=e}},4),n.link("../../../../components/richString/RichString",{RichString(e){m=e}},5),n.link("../../LocaleContext",{useLocale(e){d=e}},6),n.link("./inclusiveDesignFeaturesPanelStrings.json",{default(e){p=e}},7),n.link("../../simulations/browseAndFilter/BrowseAndFilterPage",{getFilterPageUrl(e){h=e}},8),n.link("../prototypes/InclusiveDesignPrototypesPanel",{InclusiveDesignPrototypesPanel(e){g=e}},9),n.link("../../../../../api/inclusiveDesign/inclusiveDesign",{InclusiveDesign(e){y=e}},10);let f=e=>i.createElement("div",{className:"feature-section-header"},i.createElement("div",{className:"title"},i.createElement("img",{src:`${a}/${e.icon}`,className:"title-icon",alt:""}),i.createElement("h2",{className:"h2"},e.titleString)),i.createElement("p",{cl